<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/intermediate/the-moon-and-eclipses">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The Moon is the sky's great teacher — close enough to watch its geometry play out monthly. Its
    <strong>phases</strong> are not Earth's shadow (the most common misconception in astronomy)
    but simple viewing angles of a half-lit ball. Its <strong>eclipses</strong>, though, are
    shadows — spectacular ones, possible only because the Sun is 400× wider than the Moon and
    also 400× farther away, making the two discs almost exactly the same size in our sky.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four facts that explain the Moon's entire public life" widget="equationStep" config={moonCfg} />

  <h2>What the Moon has done for Earth</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stabilised the axis:</strong> the Moon's gravity keeps Earth's 23.4° tilt steady over millions of years; without it, chaotic tilts would swing climates wildly (Mars, without a big moon, has wobbled by tens of degrees).</li>
    <li><strong>Slowed the spin:</strong> tidal friction lengthens Earth's day by ~2 milliseconds per century — 1.4 billion years ago, a day lasted about 18 hours.</li>
    <li><strong>Receded measurably:</strong> laser retroreflectors left by Apollo astronauts bounce beams back; the Moon retreats 3.8 cm per year. Direct measurement of tidal evolution.</li>
    <li><strong>A record of the early solar system:</strong> no weather, no plate tectonics — the Apollo rocks (382 kg, 1969–72) still anchor the dating of the whole inner solar system.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NASA Apollo Lunar Surface Journal; OpenStax, Astronomy 2e (The Moon and Tides); LLR measurements (McDonald Observatory).</p>
</TopicPage>
